        "### Knapsack Problem with Dynamic Programming\n",
        "\n",
        "The **Knapsack Problem** is a classic optimization problem where you need to choose items from a set, each with a weight and a value, to fit into a knapsack with a maximum weight capacity. The goal is to maximize the total value of the items in the knapsack without exceeding its capacity.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Dynamic Programming Approach\n",
        "\n",
        "For the 0/1 Knapsack Problem (where each item can either be taken or not taken), dynamic programming is an efficient approach. We can build a table (or array) `dp` where `dp[i][w]` represents the maximum value that can be obtained with the first `i` items and a knapsack capacity of `w`.\n",
        "\n",
        "The recurrence relation is as follows:\n",
        "\n",
        "*   If `weight[i-1]` (weight of the current item) is greater than `w` (current capacity), then the item cannot be included. So, `dp[i][w] = dp[i-1][w]`.\n",
        "*   If `weight[i-1]` is less than or equal to `w`, then we have two choices:\n",
        "    1.  **Exclude the current item:** The value is `dp[i-1][w]`.\n",
        "    2.  **Include the current item:** The value is `value[i-1]` + `dp[i-1][w - weight[i-1]]`.\n",
        "    We take the maximum of these two choices: `dp[i][w] = max(dp[i-1][w], value[i-1] + dp[i-1][w - weight[i-1]])`.\n",
        "\n",
        "Base cases: `dp[0][w] = 0` (no items) and `dp[i][0] = 0` (no capacity)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "789bedcf",
        "outputId": "98122960-6525-47b5-e173-50d4325a4d8a"
